\f0\fs24 \cf0 ###student_prompt\
\
An artificial membrane-bound structure modeling a plant cell was filled with a solution containing a fixed concentration of total dissolved solutes. Replicate samples of these synthetic tissue structures were weighed to determine their initial mass and then placed into beakers containing sucrose solutions of varying concentrations ($0.0\\text\{ M\}$, $0.2\\text\{ M\}$, $0.4\\text\{ M\}$, $0.6\\text\{ M\}$, and $0.8\\text\{ M\}$) at a constant temperature of $22^\\circ\\text\{C\}$. After $60\\text\{ minutes\}$, the synthetic tissue samples were removed, blotted dry, and reweighed to determine their final mass. The mean percent change in mass for the samples at each external sucrose concentration was calculated.\
\
Construct a graph on the axes provided to represent the relationship between the external sucrose concentration and the mean percent change in mass of the synthetic tissue samples. Draw a single straight line of best fit through the data points to illustrate the overall trend. Using your graph, determine the internal osmolarity (solute concentration) of the synthetic tissue samples by identifying the point where no net movement of water occurs. Report this concentration value with appropriate units in the space provided.\
\
---\
\
### synthetic_data_source\
\
The dataset is constructed using a linear relationship describing water potential equilibration where percent mass change ($\\Delta M$) depends on the external sucrose concentration ($C$) via the true generative formula:\
\
\
$$\\Delta M = -35.0 \\cdot C + 10.5$$\
\
To simulate real experimental variation without losing exact decimal reproducibility, three explicit synthetic replicates ($R_1, R_2, R_3$) were generated for each concentration group. The final displayed mean is computed as the exact arithmetic mean of these three values:\
\
$$\\text\{Mean\} = \\frac\{R_1 + R_2 + R_3\}\{3\}$$\
\
#### Replicate Values and Arithmetic Verifications:\
\
* **$0.0\\text\{ M\}$ Sucrose:**\
* Replicates: $11.2\\%$, $9.8\\%$, $10.5\\%$\
* Arithmetic: $\\frac\{11.2 + 9.8 + 10.5\}\{3\} = \\frac\{31.5\}\{3\} = 10.50 \\rightarrow$ Rounded to one decimal: **$10.5\\%$**\
\
\
* **$0.2\\text\{ M\}$ Sucrose:**\
* Replicates: $3.1\\%$, $4.0\\%$, $3.4\\%$\
* Arithmetic: $\\frac\{3.1 + 4.0 + 3.4\}\{3\} = \\frac\{10.5\}\{3\} = 3.50 \\rightarrow$ Rounded to one decimal: **$3.5\\%$**\
\
\
* **$0.4\\text\{ M\}$ Sucrose:**\
* Replicates: $-3.3\\%$, $-3.8\\%$, $-3.4\\%$\
* Arithmetic: $\\frac\{-3.3 + -3.8 + -3.4\}\{3\} = \\frac\{-10.5\}\{3\} = -3.50 \\rightarrow$ Rounded to one decimal: **$-3.5\\%$**\
\
\
* **$0.6\\text\{ M\}$ Sucrose:**\
* Replicates: $-10.1\\%$, $-11.0\\%$, $-10.4\\%$\
* Arithmetic: $\\frac\{-10.1 + -11.0 + -10.4\}\{3\} = \\frac\{-31.5\}\{3\} = -10.50 \\rightarrow$ Rounded to one decimal: **$-10.5\\%$**\
\
\
* **$0.8\\text\{ M\}$ Sucrose:**\
* Replicates: $-17.2\\%$, $-17.9\\%$, $-17.4\\%$\
* Arithmetic: $\\frac\{-17.2 + -17.9 + -17.4\}\{3\} = \\frac\{-52.5\}\{3\} = -17.50 \\rightarrow$ Rounded to one decimal: **$-17.5\\%$**\
\
